WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.840
Retrieval augmented generation brings external documents into model context.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:14.580
The pipeline may ingest files, chunk text, attach metadata, create embeddings, store vectors, rank results, and insert selected passages into a prompt.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.080
Each step must preserve provenance and authorization.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:25.740
If a document's access rules are lost during ingestion or indexing, the retrieval system may later expose content to users who should not see it.

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:29.720
Access-control metadata should travel with the document and its chunks.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:40.580
Tenant, organization, project, classification, owner, source system, timestamp, retention status, and deletion status can all matter.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.840
Retrieval should filter before content enters the prompt, not after the model has already seen it.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:52.940
Tenant separation and object-level checks help prevent one user's question from retrieving another user's content through a shared index.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:56.600
Retrieved instructions and retrieved evidence should be separated.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:59.620
A policy document may be evidence about a policy.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:02.400
A support ticket may be evidence about a case.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:07.900
A random document should not be able to become a system instruction because it contains imperative language.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:16.120
Source attribution, citations, document age, and confidence signals help users evaluate whether an answer is grounded in appropriate evidence.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:23.380
RAG systems also need safe behavior when evidence is insufficient, stale, conflicting, or suspicious.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:32.240
The application should be able to decline to answer, ask for clarification, show uncertainty, or escalate to a human rather than inventing certainty.

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:38.620
Ranking and context selection should be monitored because retrieval failures can turn into confident but unsupported answers.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:43.080
Safety comes from the full retrieval lifecycle, not only from the final prompt.
